17592186129386 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186362442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186362444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186362447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186362451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186362454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Volumes of Solids Using Triple Integrals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1050 1380.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129386 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8ed94446-a68c-54d6-8877-20405d94dc8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129703 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129703 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129703 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the $xy$-plane, the surface $z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and the planes $x=0,$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ $y=0,$ $y=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129703 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d60206f-2159-50c2-97e6-e576ed41d216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129703 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129766 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129766 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129766 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129766 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the $yz$-plane, the surface $x=6yz+12,$ and the planes $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129766 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fac0ad3-f7f8-517d-a7fd-3686f90064e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129766 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 6yz+12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{6yz+12}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=0}^{x=6yz+12}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6yz+12)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6yz+12\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[3y{z}^{2}+12z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3y(1)^{2}+12(1))-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y+12\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+12y]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2}-0)+12(2-0) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130098 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130098 #edb/ref 1061 657225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130098 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f8517a18-fa1d-5284-b1cb-8c87ef3c4a7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130098 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130099 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130099 #edb/ref 1061 657226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130099 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5606b802-fe70-5ed7-9a0d-af46a1e7bdb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130099 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130100 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186362451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130100 #edb/ref 1061 657230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130100 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10a0d833-bae3-51f0-86fa-4e40e909b391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130100 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130101 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186362454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130101 #edb/ref 1061 657235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130101 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "920555d7-5a28-5187-9ea9-a47f47e41429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130101 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130410 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the double Integral of the function $f(x,y) = 1$ over a domain $D\\subset \\mathbb R^2$ is simply the area of $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\ \\!(D) = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 1 \\cdot \\textrm d A = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}  \\ \\textrm d A\n$$\n\nWe have a similar result for triple integrals:\n\n*If $R$ is a region in three-dimensional space, then the volume of $R$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Volume}(R) = \\iiint\\limits_{R} \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the volume of $R$ equals the triple integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=1$ over $R.$ \n\nFor example, let's use triple integrals to find the volume of the rectangular prism shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8f9ab5db03f1cdf84ef484aca1ab89995237e7ef333c9b7c563d0080d317e981.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written in set-builder notation as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}dx\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dy\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dz \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We get the same value using the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\text{length}\\cdot \\text{width}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130410 #edb/ref 1065 4911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130410 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8b8f4748-190d-5fda-9a83-139e6a994d94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130410 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234975 #edb/ref 1069 "e-26133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234975 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the solid enclosed between the paraboloids ![](images/df/df720fc5b6471db8190154ad9e8c1a3094f3fe885f6150de21bb7a5ef0653be5.svg) and ![](images/ea/ea8dbe9b1e82824b527a7f87153b0ef9aa33e6af2f9f48ff5377b4ad19b48c2b.svg) whose volume can be written in terms of double integrals, as follows:\n![](images/7c/7cf42f88ef110c2dea6d3ab150ed095c8845a4b3a110ae955e1c1da666bcc364.svg)\n\nFind ![](images/46/4694851f2a7762e81f5ff1328018557fee878a018c60f2b8db8be5b9775f24ee.svg) ![](images/65/65537e63a2f6c671139d4145c5157b89d95c0f6db5a0304d39f3aa0ee781a4a1.svg) and ![](images/ae/ae992fcbb9b6e797b7a06dda4dbedec893ebad102944c504a0de22790e75e940.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4031c8a9-0559-5d8d-a1a5-6e3402c19b27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234975 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the ![](images/d4/d43a228fcfe8f3eeb60a2ce9ec2d0cca2ebd281c09cceef580587e8ef5672511.svg)-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n![](images/f9/f94b2d4acca3dae2e05ff1cc1ba860488de8ca4fd3b1468bf2f0287ac8fd208b.svg)\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the ![](images/d4/d43a228fcfe8f3eeb60a2ce9ec2d0cca2ebd281c09cceef580587e8ef5672511.svg)-plane is a circle.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type III region:\n![](images/28/28f530c26d2a007471d7419a705b33a62378348c0bbfa9fab6f79e2aa0fa5fb2.svg)\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the interior of the circle ![](images/1d/1d4b64030885f51b4f44e80010686c07bdb3e8d88386271543d08b91e6ad9d75.svg)\n\nAs a result, the volume of ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) is given by\n![](images/00/0066a0d710b1f0a807ed0ece319744c6d826d0f7af56f8db4b88d8cb8ea3f652.svg)\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) consists of\n\n- the lower part ![](images/68/687143de30f2e07f089e3e2d728d31db586a552ab6e9694dccb0612e0b6b6fa9.svg) and\n\n- the upper part ![](images/a1/a150d3381edb05cce1fdbbaf82b7364009114579fe1b0e647d004bff51b47d75.svg)\n\nfor ![](images/22/225fcd90b7a79b6f4559894a4ec6ffd00aa643ee0c83719e6ebd165764497f81.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n![](images/11/11b70e034ca321a0eeabaeac26e55564ef04951147a69d158a23795b4cd0d0c9.svg)\n\nSo,\n![](images/df/dfe19692ea883c7962ae2f7b539263c064afbab0012d5e9238f0046384de56b7.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Double-Integral Formula for Volume" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186234975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235284 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "13f9ed67-24d8-5d41-8f82-1296abc57f12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362409 #edb/ref 1064 "When a solid is described in words, we first identify its bounding surfaces and then decide how to describe the entire region with inequalities.\n\nA useful strategy is:\n\n- identify the variable that lies between a lower surface and an upper surface,\n\n- determine the projection of the solid onto a coordinate plane,\n\n- write bounds for the projected region,\n\n- use those bounds to set up a triple integral with integrand equal to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg).\n\nIn many volume problems, the solid lies between a bottom surface\n![](images/8e/8eeea9a9caa745d87342df718b249603d257e35820bea2df1f1d647d61877c0c.svg)\nand a top surface\n![](images/21/211a455fb9c563450b5b5225f0d3615467c89870e467920ad73beacb802375b8.svg)\n\nIn that case, we describe the solid by\n![](images/eb/eb36f1bb66ca8c06c73de972395684c368cc3cdba4d05b9300dee9f852c3174b.svg)\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the projection of the solid onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nSo, to find the volume, we use\n![](images/c6/c6fd71393350dac5cd3d1067227863cab1e92534812ea083231408ce660143bf.svg)\n\nSo, the verbal description tells us two things:\n\n- which surface is below and which is above,\n\n- which points ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) belong to the base region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg).\n\nBefore integrating, it is always a good idea to check that the upper surface is actually above the lower surface on the entire projected region." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362409 #edb/ref 1065 15980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362409 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8f80f606-7a23-56be-8c78-48b9eeaa0fd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362409 #edb/ref 1067 "Interpreting a Verbal Description of a Solid" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362410 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the volume of the solid region enclosed by the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane, the plane ![](images/af/af57fba1cdb37044c8a4f922ba0ce95fcae5347960e050ac391af840198aca28.svg) and the planes ![](images/89/89c97ad93c125fc2b6f61d44fe9a90845fd289329d79d506c857f970d7586540.svg) ![](images/48/480183a1321e5b4fdff9500a551818f6fb0ab2713c0fdd8fa9df88a640394af9.svg) ![](images/8d/8de04eef03bf4f5fd455bcd39f1d747bc5e38650a1f8c6dd5249166cf5fc022d.svg) and ![](images/ca/ca7dadeea1df352335554e1c48b6398167035636994354744737e4c75a1b0134.svg)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region.\n\nFirst, the phrase **enclosed by the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane** tells us that the bottom of the solid is\n![](images/ef/efcfb7a56c85727ded8a9d5c9bd75fd79ac0bc20ba202c2da6aaca3c4e3ba503.svg)\n\nThe plane\n![](images/21/211cfe0529803c8450173dbcaa60c66f276389b5944047bc390738703f657309.svg)\nis the top of the solid.\n\nAlso, the planes\n![](images/a3/a3fe77f111727e68d20276851f67f7fc662eaea5df49eaf96695a9234f6304bf.svg)\ndescribe the projection of the solid onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane. So the base region is the rectangle\n![](images/59/5925cb0d118a2a3457f4683c7b38bfa59bc5e8bbc106af32f0364a0207f9af25.svg)\n\nTherefore, for each point ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) in this rectangle, the variable ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) runs from the bottom surface to the top surface:\n![](images/b1/b1af88023edd0b854bb6b598521693ebf4e941d8a5567e6801801f1deeacb902.svg)\n\nSo the volume is\n![](images/bd/bd9acbbd0fb9472aafdc28fe38d7cf6a103c123ff999116cefca75f8dbf624a8.svg)\n\nNow we evaluate:\n\n![](images/fa/fa67106261e7d847a0be115b74ca3ebaa5d5aff7ceca30b0b94b950fb1b741cd.svg)\n\nTherefore, the volume of the solid is ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)\n\nIn problems like this, the planes involving only ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) usually describe the base region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane, while the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane and the surface or plane involving ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) describe the lower and upper bounds for ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362410 #edb/ref 1065 15981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362410 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca3b6881-db7f-536a-b48b-77adde0a1b19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362410 #edb/ref 1067 "Setting Up Volume from a Verbal Description" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362411 #edb/ref 1064 "Alternatively, we can conceptualize the volume of a solid using a **triple integral**. \n\nRevisiting the previous example enclosed between the paraboloids, our solid can be formally written as a 3D region:\n![](images/17/17cee1ab33ee8dcd73e0c3a0151c563a6c64ca1c898383dbbca4d8ac7f1e0ff5.svg)\n\nThis means that for each point ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) in the projection region ![](images/87/87e7e0743ecd6609730f4b9a9b3681b1f360ca86bcb209e03561a197b18782f3.svg) the variable ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) runs from the lower surface \n![](images/6a/6a37b724b57c8f5650e5970d1f90da49fe6a482582c7776aa567d41c2f6c406b.svg)\nup to the upper surface \n![](images/58/585d8d094c7eea9f1d7b242bebd015c27b39692e2cddbbaa76c0a87ce65b3ea5.svg)\n\nThe volume of ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) is given by evaluating a triple integral of ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) over the region. By computing the innermost integral with respect to ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg), this calculation logically reduces to the double integral formula:\n![](images/8e/8e024d3e6c21346148c18139918dc5780683039bb34749489e188740ec3fa836.svg)\n\nIf we write ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) using ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) as the outer variable, then\n![](images/d4/d4cdea1370f4184ff8a5387dd95ed1cedd1b81d6027c32eea9240549d0dff0a4.svg)\nso\n![](images/10/109e5d163609178b9c69ae87bd236d3e0a8830157556082f92f2933c8b6d215b.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362411 #edb/ref 1065 15983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362411 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ea755805-d530-504f-965f-afb35bf9a5b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362411 #edb/ref 1067 "Connecting Triple Integrals and Double Integrals for Volume" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362412 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes a solid is enclosed between two surfaces. In these cases, we can compute its volume using a double integral over the projection region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nSuppose a solid is enclosed between the paraboloids\n![](images/7b/7b63c2c4326ca1b7c5001ce0fbed7a7f0e4c5f54a8b4b26eca446f2b2b65dfa5.svg)\n\nTo compute its volume, we use\n![](images/83/83fed741dc600a6aa368543b2522f90191579e318c653b03a6924fc9ffa1868d.svg)\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the projection region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nFirst, we find where the two surfaces intersect. This gives the boundary of the projection region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane:\n![](images/3d/3d710d141ba98ae84f8c82b8ba0233781fa3627b639857710981c6c657fac203.svg)\n\nSo, the projection region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the disk\n![](images/d5/d5eef3331fa2e621c6a523f562fc759540299b92a24dfb1502b0d73c11377157.svg)\n\nThe height of the solid is the difference between the upper surface (![](images/cd/cdac8cf874dd0689e35a1c8d1a790bd5279d33ca80f0fd6fb4a2034e4a8b35f4.svg)) and the lower surface (![](images/69/6963e5988546516a47de0a33528be8c2c1b4626d97d9730e8bb950aa9100fe92.svg)):\n![](images/50/50c6958184049a24a56265775b5500b62bac9face27b2893824ed7d1b58d70ec.svg)\n\nIf we write ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) using ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) as the outer variable, then\n![](images/67/671080e239a3f478bcd6b47f0b6b31bdd3bef18de65edc92ac956d4a5cf07683.svg)\n\nThe volume is given by the iterated integral:\n![](images/18/180f43f3029d64ca0fe7971d108167a900d05ddbaa9aed0e017e3ef85cf2c279.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362412 #edb/ref 1065 15982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362412 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "efa958a5-e1ae-5402-b9a3-980b67367758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362412 #edb/ref 1067 "Using a Projection Region to Set Up a Double Integral" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362442 #edb/ref 1061 657240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362442 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00c1d246-4d5e-5f8e-b7d4-2c141141c41f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362442 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362444 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362444 #edb/ref 1061 657234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362444 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "151541fd-37a6-5cc8-a5fc-bc2d3ff14d79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362444 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362447 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186362442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362447 #edb/ref 1061 657239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362447 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8287853b-9ed9-5b6c-be64-14f1c8c00238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362447 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362451 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186362444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362451 #edb/ref 1061 657233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362451 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c05a1568-2d05-5343-8282-ea2d803952c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362451 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362454 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186362447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362454 #edb/ref 1061 657238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362454 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e257